"""Integrate the executed external-site evidence into the final manuscript."""
from pathlib import Path
import json,re
import pandas as pd

def extend(text,root):
    from write_manuscript import table
    r=root/'external_results';m=pd.read_csv(r/'external_metrics.csv');z=m[m.group=='all'].set_index('model')
    rows=[]
    for n,label in [('persistence','Persistence'),('mean24','24-hour mean'),('seasonal24','Previous day'),('hgb_flow','HGB flow/calendar'),('hgb_rain','HGB flow/rain'),('hgb_oracle','HGB oracle')]:
        rows.append([label,f'{z.loc[n,"MAE_m3"]:,.1f}',f'{z.loc[n,"RMSE_m3"]:,.1f}',f'{z.loc[n,"R2"]:.3f}',f'{100*z.loc[n,"MAE_over_mean_target"]:.1f}'])
    t4=table(['Configuration','MAE (m³)','RMSE (m³)','R²','MAE/mean (%)'],rows)
    abstract='''Wastewater inflow forecasting requires accurate hydraulic-load estimates and reliable uncertainty statements. We audited open observations from a Danish treatment plant and extended the evaluation to Köln-Weiden, Germany, using a 12-hour integrated-rate approximation of cumulative inflow. Development-only temporal cross-validation selected gradient boosting configurations, including loss and annual predictors. Temporal origins, matched configurations, input delays and residual intervals were evaluated. On the Danish 2,160-window test, historical-input boosting achieved mean absolute error (MAE) of 2,280 m³, compared with 3,407 m³ for a 24-hour mean and 7,607 m³ for the legacy configuration. Historical meteorology did not improve accuracy consistently across Danish periods. On the German 6,588-window test, flow/rain boosting achieved MAE of 1,662 m³, compared with 1,900 m³ for flow/calendar boosting and 3,293 m³ for the mean baseline. Thus, the incremental value of historical rain was site-dependent. Nominal 90% pooled intervals for the historical models covered 83.7% overall versus 21.0% of high-volume windows in Denmark, and 92.6% versus 39.3% in Germany. Rolling calibration improved Danish aggregate coverage but reduced German coverage to 88.5%. High-volume windows comprised five Danish and 41 German threshold-defined episodes. These retrospective findings support configuration audits and separate evaluation of average accuracy and hydraulic-tail reliability. The German models were refitted locally; zero-shot transfer and operational treatment benefits were not established. Versioned data, predictions and executable checks support computational reproduction.'''
    if not 200<=len(abstract.split())<=250:raise ValueError('External abstract length')
    text=re.sub(r'(## Abstract\n\n).*?(\n\n## Keywords)',lambda m:m[1]+abstract+m[2],text,flags=re.S)
    changes={
      'an open-data temporal audit':'a two-site open-data audit',
      '- Historical meteorology did not provide a consistent incremental improvement.':'- Historical rainfall gains and rolling calibration behavior differed by site.',
      '- Flow-based boosting improved 12-hour MAE across three temporal test periods.':'- A German-site evaluation added 6,588 test windows and 41 high-volume episodes.',
      'The contribution is a traceable audit of an integrated inflow target: matched configuration comparisons, development-selected information sets, multiple temporal test periods, maturity-aware residual updates and episode-level diagnostics.':'The contribution is a traceable audit of an integrated inflow target: matched configuration comparisons, development-selected information sets, multiple temporal test periods, maturity-aware residual updates and episode-level diagnostics, extended to a second independently sourced site.',
      'All revision analyses were formulated after the original results had been examined, and this reuse of the dataset is explicit throughout.':'The Danish revision was formulated after its original results had been examined. An external-site extension was documented before computing German forecast errors. Both use archived observations and are retrospective; neither was independently preregistered.',
      'independent measurements and prospective validation are needed to assess generality and operational value.':'prospective operational validation and more sites are needed to assess broader generality and decision value.'}
    for old,new in changes.items():
        if old not in text:raise ValueError('Missing manuscript patch: '+old[:80])
        text=text.replace(old,new)
    method='''### 2.7. External-site methodological replication

To address reliance on one plant, we added the Köln-Weiden archive from Sonnenschein and Ziel, DOI 10.5281/zenodo.6992694, version 1.0 [10], associated with the prior intraday forecasting study [3]. This is a previously published dataset, not new field sampling. Its CC BY 4.0 license and provider MD5 checksum were verified. The CSV documents flow in L/s and rainfall rate in mm/min at 15-minute intervals. It contains 105,120 timestamps from 2017 through 2019, with three missing values in each of these two variables and no duplicated or irregular timestamps.

We retained the archive's uniform, timezone-unspecified clock and do not label it UTC. Each hourly value ending at t required four finite nonnegative samples in (t-1 hour,t]. Mean flow was multiplied by 3.6 to obtain m³/h; rainfall rates were summed and multiplied by 15 minutes to obtain hourly mm. These are integrated-rate approximations rather than independently verified volumetric meter readings. Right-closed aggregation avoids using samples after the issue time. No interpolation or transport of the Danish low/flat screening thresholds was applied. Available sewer-level variables were not added, so the historical information comparison remained flow/calendar versus flow/calendar/rain; other Danish meteorological variables were unavailable.

Development covered 2017-2018, calibration January-March 2019, and testing April-December 2019, with the same horizon purges. We fixed the 12-hour target, baselines, 16-candidate HGB grid, development-fold selection and three interval methods before calculating external errors. Models were fitted on German development observations; this evaluates the approach at an independent site and is not zero-shot transfer of a Danish trained model. Matched diagnostics fixed seven leaves and L2=1 while varying loss/annual features, and compared fixed absolute-loss/no-annual information sets. MAE divided by the site's mean test target was reported to contextualize scale; m³ errors were not pooled across sites. The separately archived radar-forecast file was inspected but not used: its numbered columns were not verified to constitute a complete operational 12-hour forecast with known availability times.

'''
    text=text.replace('## 3. Results\n',method+'## 3. Results\n')
    external='''### 3.6. Evidence from the German site

The external evaluation retained 17,484 development, 2,124 calibration and 6,588 test issues. The mean German test target was 7,846.3 m³, compared with 16,447.4 m³ at the Danish primary test. The German development-derived high-volume threshold was 14,099.0 m³, exceeded by 700 test windows in 41 contiguous episodes. This substantially increases episode representation relative to the Danish five episodes, but does not establish meteorological independence or population-wide generality.

Flow/rain HGB achieved MAE 1,661.9 m³ versus 1,899.8 m³ for flow/calendar HGB and 3,293.1 m³ for the mean baseline (Table 4). The rain-versus-flow paired difference was -238.0 m³, with a 48-hour bootstrap sensitivity range of -338.3 to -154.2 m³; the matched fixed-configuration difference was -239.3 m³. Unlike the Danish primary result, historical rain improved accuracy here. The selected rain model's MAE/mean-target ratio was 21.2%; the corresponding Danish historical-model ratio was 13.9%. This difference is descriptive, with different sites, periods and available covariates.

**Table 4. External-site 12-hour test performance at Köln-Weiden (n=6,588).** Models were trained locally using 2017-2018 observations. MAE/mean is MAE divided by the mean test target, not MAPE. Oracle rainfall is non-operational.

'''+t4+'''

The loss/calendar effect also differed by site. With seven leaves and L2=1, German squared-loss MAE was 1,866.3 m³ with annual predictors and 1,929.1 m³ without them; absolute-loss MAE was 1,666.7 and 1,660.5 m³, respectively. The large Danish deterioration from including annual predictors under squared loss therefore did not recur in Germany. This supports configuration sensitivity as a reason to audit, rather than a universal rule to remove annual predictors.

For German flow/rain HGB, pooled intervals covered 92.6% overall but 39.3% of high-volume windows. Wet/dry calibration gave 90.6% and 54.1%, whereas rolling calibration gave 88.5% and 32.9%. Mean widths were 12,852, 8,504 and 9,145 m³, respectively. Thus rolling adaptation did not uniformly improve reliability. The oracle had pooled coverage of 94.3% overall and 68.3% in the high-volume subgroup, despite its small MAE of 832.6 m³. The external evidence supports concern about aggregate versus tail reliability while showing that the best calibration response is site-dependent.

'''
    ep=root/'external_clean/reproduction.json'
    if ep.exists():
        rep=json.loads(ep.read_text());external+=f"A separate clean-environment rerun reproduced all {rep['files_compared']} external CSV tables with maximum absolute numerical difference {max(t['maximum_absolute_numeric_difference'] for t in rep['tables']):.1f}. Direct checks reconstructed each external calibration/test target from 48 quarter-hour flow-rate samples using the documented unit conversion.\n\n"
    text=text.replace('## 4. Discussion\n',external+'## 4. Discussion\n')
    text=text.replace('Further temporal tests reduce reliance on one split, but cannot replace an untouched future period or an independent plant.','Further Danish temporal tests reduce reliance on one split, while the German extension addresses site specificity through local refitting. Neither design replaces a prospective operating-period evaluation.')
    text=text.replace('The consistent oracle advantage at 12 hours establishes room for improved information under the evaluated modelling conditions.','The oracle advantage at both sites establishes room for improved information under the evaluated modelling conditions. The observed historical-rain advantage in Germany also shows that the Danish null comparison is not a general statement about rainfall information.')
    text=text.replace('Sparse, overlapping episodes prevent reliable estimation of rare-event performance and prohibit treating 81 windows as 81 independent hydraulic events.','Sparse, overlapping Danish episodes prevent reliable estimation of rare-event performance and prohibit treating 81 windows as 81 independent hydraulic events. The German 700 high-volume windows in 41 episodes broaden the evidence but still do not represent 700 independent events.')
    text=text.replace('For this dataset, flow-based boosting is a credible 12-hour predictive benchmark, while high-volume residual intervals require further work.','Across both evaluated sites, flow-based boosting is a credible 12-hour predictive benchmark, while high-volume residual intervals require further work. Historical rainfall and rolling calibration should be re-evaluated locally because their incremental performance differed between sites.')
    start=text.index('The study concerns one anonymized plant',text.index('## 6. Limitations'))
    end=text.index('## 7. Conclusions',start)
    text=text[:start]+'''The evaluation covers two plants with different record lengths and measurement documentation, not a representative sample of treatment systems. The Danish plant is anonymized and has approximately 15 months of incomplete observations; its effective development history is shorter. The German archive spans three years but does not establish the time zone or real sensor-availability times. Integrated volumes are approximated from rate samples; Danish aggregation semantics remain unconfirmed. Complete-window selection and heuristic Danish screening can affect representativeness. The unscreened refit does not eliminate missingness bias.

The Danish revision reuses previously inspected outcomes. The external design was documented before its model errors were calculated, but is still a retrospective, non-preregistered evaluation selected after the Danish audit. Local German refitting is not zero-shot transfer, and differences between sites cannot isolate the effect of climate, catchment, record length or sensing. The learning family and search remain limited. No mechanistic comparator, prospective control trial or fully verified operational weather-forecast replay was performed. Network levels at the German site do not by themselves provide storage geometry, action constraints or treatment endpoints. Threshold-defined episodes may remain dependent. Bootstrap ranges are assumption-sensitive, and no globally unique method or guaranteed publication readiness is claimed.

'''+text[end:]
    oldstart=text.index('On the DHI open dataset,',text.index('## 7. Conclusions'));oldend=text.index('## Data Availability Statement',oldstart)
    text=text[:oldstart]+'''Loss and annual-predictor choices materially changed the interpretation of the Danish wastewater inflow benchmark. Development-selected historical HGB reduced its primary 12-hour MAE to 2,280 m³ compared with 3,407 m³ for a 24-hour mean and 7,607 m³ for the legacy configuration. Three further Danish origins and a German-site evaluation supported the usefulness of flow-based 12-hour prediction, but historical rainfall gains and calendar sensitivity were site-dependent. In Germany, flow/rain HGB achieved MAE 1,662 m³ versus 3,293 m³ for the mean baseline. Near-nominal aggregate interval coverage coexisted with high-volume undercoverage at both sites; rolling calibration improved some Danish measures but deteriorated in Germany. The complete computational evidence is openly reproducible. Prospective forecast availability, additional sites and operational decision endpoints remain necessary before claiming treatment or control benefits.

'''+text[oldend:]
    text=text.replace('No new measurements were collected. Users must retain the source license and attribution; non-commercial terms remain applicable to data reuse.',
      'The external observations are available from Sonnenschein and Ziel [10], DOI [10.5281/zenodo.6992694](https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.6992694), under CC BY 4.0. Both sources and transformations are identified separately. No new measurements were collected. Users must retain each source license and attribution; the Danish non-commercial terms are not imposed on the independently licensed German source.')
    text+='''
10. Sonnenschein, B., and Ziel, F. (2022). *Wastewater Treatment Plant Inflow Rate, Network Water Levels and Rain Rate for Koeln Weiden, Germany*, version 1.0. Zenodo. [doi:10.5281/zenodo.6992694](https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.6992694).
'''
    return text
